WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club

﻿1

00:00:33.243 --> 00:00:34.945
- Oh....


00:00:36.747 --> 00:00:39.082
Oh...


00:00:39.116 --> 00:00:41.452
- Come on, come on,
go, go, go, go!


00:00:41.486 --> 00:00:45.056
- Come on, we're coming through.
- Go, go. Come on. - You'll be okay.


00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:47.225
You'll be alright.
You're gonna be fine.


00:00:47.258 --> 00:00:48.726
- Thank you, Stubby.


00:00:52.097 --> 00:00:53.665
- Well done, boy.
- Good boy.


00:00:53.698 --> 00:00:55.667
- He saved him again.


00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.403
That's a good dog.


00:01:36.644 --> 00:01:39.046
- Get out of here,
you stinkin' mutt!


00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.184
Go rifle through
someone else's trash.


00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:46.722
Go!


00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.430
- Ah! Watch it, mutt.


00:01:54.464 --> 00:01:57.132
- Hey!
- Come on!


00:01:57.165 --> 00:01:59.435
Whose dog is that?!


00:02:35.874 --> 00:02:38.543
Take care!
We're proud of you!


00:02:45.484 --> 00:02:49.855
When my brother put on the uniform
of the United States Army,


00:02:49.889 --> 00:02:52.525
I was so very proud.


00:02:52.558 --> 00:02:56.863
In 1917, our country went
to war with Germany.


00:02:56.897 --> 00:02:58.631
All across the nation,


00:02:58.664 --> 00:03:01.768
thousands of young men answered
the call to fight.


00:03:01.802 --> 00:03:03.470
They were just boys.


00:03:03.503 --> 00:03:06.206
Well, they were called Doughboys,


00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:09.342
and my brother Robert was one of them.


00:03:10.744 --> 00:03:13.915
By the time we joined the war
in April 1917,


00:03:13.948 --> 00:03:17.885
the French and British had already been
fighting the mighty German Army


00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:19.822
for three long years.


00:03:19.855 --> 00:03:21.889
Their spirits were low.


00:03:21.923 --> 00:03:25.727
But they were happy to know
that we were coming to help.


00:03:25.761 --> 00:03:29.298
This is the true story
of a special friendship


00:03:29.331 --> 00:03:32.601
my brother made while training
for the war.


00:03:44.781 --> 00:03:47.250
- Oh, no, you don't.


00:03:47.283 --> 00:03:49.886
Scram, mutt!


00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:51.354
Scram!


00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:56.927
- Left! Left! Left, right, left!


00:03:56.961 --> 00:03:59.363
Platoon... halt!


00:03:59.396 --> 00:04:01.866
Left... face!


00:04:01.899 --> 00:04:03.400
At ease!


00:04:07.471 --> 00:04:09.608
No time for lip spittle, boys.


00:04:09.641 --> 00:04:13.445
We have four short weeks left
to turn you into soldiers.


00:04:13.478 --> 00:04:16.982
You've worked hard on drill,
learned teamwork,


00:04:17.014 --> 00:04:18.750
discipline. But...


00:04:18.783 --> 00:04:20.819
next is the crump-hole.


00:04:20.852 --> 00:04:24.189
That's why you and I are here.


00:04:32.331 --> 00:04:34.400
- Wha...?! How did this dog
get in here?!


00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:36.836
Get out! Get out of here!


00:04:37.603 --> 00:04:38.906
Hey!!


00:04:38.939 --> 00:04:40.439
- Ah!


00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:44.678
Hey, shhh, shhh.


00:04:44.711 --> 00:04:47.881
Get off of me. Come on, go. I don't have
any more food. Go away. Shoo.


00:04:47.915 --> 00:04:49.983
Come on, you're gonna get me in trouble.
Get off.


00:04:50.017 --> 00:04:53.253
- Now General Pershing's gonna change all that.
- Go.


00:04:53.854 --> 00:04:55.390
Shoo. Shoo.


00:04:55.423 --> 00:04:57.658
Go. Get away.


00:04:59.827 --> 00:05:01.829
- When we get there,


00:05:01.863 --> 00:05:05.400
we're gonna take the fight to the enemy.


00:05:08.002 --> 00:05:09.737
At least he's ready.


00:05:11.941 --> 00:05:14.977
This pooch belong to any of you men?


00:05:20.783 --> 00:05:23.353
I see you eyeballing him, Private Conroy.


00:05:23.386 --> 00:05:25.754
Better fetch that dog a bone.


00:05:25.788 --> 00:05:27.691
Looks like he could use it.


00:05:28.992 --> 00:05:31.895
Platoon, atten...tion!


00:05:35.398 --> 00:05:37.000
Dismissed.


00:05:37.033 --> 00:05:40.638
- Alright. Come on, boy,
you heard the sergeant. Let's go.


00:05:40.671 --> 00:05:44.575
And that's how Robert met
his new little buddy.


00:05:45.977 --> 00:05:49.280
- Come on, move along.
This is not a picnic.


00:05:52.084 --> 00:05:54.485
Oh, it's that mutt again.


00:05:54.518 --> 00:05:58.523
- Sergeant says get him fed.
- Uh, I don't cook for dogs.


00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:01.660
- Hey, Biscuit, he might be the only one
that likes your cooking.


00:06:01.693 --> 00:06:03.328
Better be nice to him.


00:06:06.932 --> 00:06:09.802
- You really know how to make friends,
don't you, Olsen?


00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:12.337
- Sure do. Always.


00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:14.841
You, uh, find a new buddy, Conroy?


00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:17.076
- No, actually, he kinda found me.


00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.911
Isn't that right, boy?


00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:21.515
- Can't believe the sergeant
let the dog stay.


00:06:21.548 --> 00:06:23.916
He must feel sorry for us.
- Alright, alright.


00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:27.420
The sergeant gave him a pass,
but the colonel never will.


00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:29.089
- Maybe the sergeant wanted a mascot,


00:06:29.123 --> 00:06:31.358
to give the unit a different look.


00:06:31.392 --> 00:06:34.428
- Ugh. A different smell, too.
Hey, no, wait!


00:06:34.461 --> 00:06:37.097
What the...? No! Bad dog! Bad dog!


00:06:37.131 --> 00:06:39.900
I hate dogs.


00:06:56.151 --> 00:06:59.722
No, no, no, no, no, no.
Hey, hey, listen.


00:06:59.755 --> 00:07:01.790
Listen. Stay.


00:07:01.823 --> 00:07:04.427
No. St...


00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:05.928
Stay.


00:07:05.962 --> 00:07:07.630
Good.


00:07:07.663 --> 00:07:11.001
You sleep there, on the floor,
not in my bed.


00:07:12.002 --> 00:07:13.502
Good.


00:07:14.504 --> 00:07:16.406
Good night, boy.


00:07:33.024 --> 00:07:36.194
- That's enough shuteye! On your feet!
Out of the sack!


00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:44.670
Left! Left! Left, right, left!


00:07:44.703 --> 00:07:47.038
Column left... march!


00:07:55.849 --> 00:08:00.854
Left! Left! Left, right, left!
Left! Left! Left, right, left!


00:08:00.887 --> 00:08:02.756
Left! Left!


00:08:05.058 --> 00:08:06.693
Go! Go! Go!


00:08:09.529 --> 00:08:12.199
Over the wall, Clayborn, not through it!


00:08:12.233 --> 00:08:14.201
- I got it.


00:08:14.235 --> 00:08:15.735
- Oh!


00:08:26.647 --> 00:08:28.717
- Whose target is that?


00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:30.652
Hey, boy, go fetch it.


00:08:32.888 --> 00:08:35.590
He's quite a dog you got there, Conroy.


00:08:35.624 --> 00:08:38.093
Not even afraid of the sound of gunfire.


00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:44.566
Well done, soldier.


00:08:44.599 --> 00:08:47.069
- Thank you, Sergeant.
- Not you.


00:08:47.103 --> 00:08:49.672
You missed the target completely.


00:08:49.705 --> 00:08:52.542
I was talking to the dog.


00:08:52.575 --> 00:08:56.546
Pick up your feet, Olsen!
One after the other!


00:08:59.616 --> 00:09:01.919
- Ow. Oh.


00:09:01.952 --> 00:09:05.590
- Back of the line, Conroy.
Do it again.


00:09:11.662 --> 00:09:15.200
Put your glasses on, Schroeder!
The enemy's that way!


00:09:22.907 --> 00:09:25.543
I loved hearing how his little dog


00:09:25.577 --> 00:09:27.780
trained right alongside Robert
and his buddies.


00:09:27.813 --> 00:09:29.614
- Platoon... halt!


00:09:29.647 --> 00:09:32.618
- Robert's letters were
full of his exploits.


00:09:32.651 --> 00:09:37.957
It made me happy to know how much joy
the dog brought to my kid brother.


00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.226
- At ease.


00:09:43.263 --> 00:09:44.798
Look at him.


00:09:44.831 --> 00:09:46.867
He has been training alongside you guys


00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:49.236
and he drills better than most of you.


00:09:50.136 --> 00:09:53.006
You could all learn something from him.


00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:08.823
- I hope you'll be able to keep him,
Conroy.


00:10:08.856 --> 00:10:12.895
- This is the army, Schroeder.
If they don't issue it, you don't need it.


00:10:12.928 --> 00:10:14.762
That includes pets.


00:10:14.795 --> 00:10:16.832
- All the same, I'm keeping him.


00:10:16.865 --> 00:10:20.168
In fact, I thought
of a name for him: Stubby.


00:10:22.271 --> 00:10:25.941
It's a perfect name
for a perfect little tail.


00:10:25.974 --> 00:10:28.278
Ain't that right? Yeah, right, Stubby?


00:10:28.311 --> 00:10:30.346
Whoa. One thing's for certain.


00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:34.717
When the colonel catches him,
it's bye-bye, doggie.


00:10:34.751 --> 00:10:37.186
- Hmm.


00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:39.355
Maybe not.


00:10:39.389 --> 00:10:41.025
Alright, bud.


00:10:41.058 --> 00:10:44.361
Listen to me, listen.
Alright, stay calm.


00:10:44.395 --> 00:10:47.197
Stay... No, listen. Sit. Sit.


00:10:47.231 --> 00:10:48.933
Stubby, come on.


00:10:48.966 --> 00:10:52.203
We're gonna stay here all night
until we get this right.


00:10:52.237 --> 00:10:55.105
Hold it... hold it.


00:10:55.139 --> 00:10:57.242
Hold... it.


00:11:06.818 --> 00:11:10.722
♪ Over there over there


00:11:10.755 --> 00:11:14.260
♪ Send the word send
the word over there ♪


00:11:14.293 --> 00:11:17.263
- That's you, men.
You're the Yankee Division.


00:11:17.697 --> 00:11:20.399
That includes you too, Stubby.


00:11:20.433 --> 00:11:23.803
- What in Sam Hill is
that dog doing here?


00:11:23.836 --> 00:11:26.139
- Platoon, attention!


00:11:26.173 --> 00:11:30.210
- This is an army base, not a petting zoo!
Whose dog is this?!


00:11:30.911 --> 00:11:33.981
- Yes, he's my dog, Colonel, sir.


00:11:39.453 --> 00:11:41.789
- What in tarnation?


00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:44.158
Did you train this dog, soldier?


00:11:44.192 --> 00:11:47.161
- Yes, sir. Wasn't that hard, sir.


00:11:47.929 --> 00:11:51.366
- Next thing you know,
he'll be barking out general orders.


00:11:51.399 --> 00:11:54.136
- Uh, sir, what would you
have us do with the dog?


00:11:54.169 --> 00:11:57.238
- Well, as long as that dog soldiers
like that,


00:11:57.271 --> 00:12:01.377
he's as welcome as any man in this army.
Carry on, men.


00:12:05.314 --> 00:12:08.784
- You're something else, Stubby.


00:12:45.857 --> 00:12:48.794
Remove your gas masks. Do it!


00:12:48.827 --> 00:12:50.362
Do it now!


00:13:01.507 --> 00:13:03.109
Outside! Now!


00:13:08.549 --> 00:13:10.249
This is teargas, men,


00:13:10.283 --> 00:13:14.188
not the mustard or poisonous gas
you will be facing.


00:13:14.221 --> 00:13:16.423
Keep your masks handy at all times.


00:13:16.457 --> 00:13:19.359
Get used to them, learn to live in them.


00:13:49.925 --> 00:13:52.294
Alright, Stubs, here we go.


00:13:52.328 --> 00:13:53.930
Whew! Oh!


00:13:58.435 --> 00:14:01.270
Oh! Okay, Stubby,
you're too quick for me, boy.


00:14:01.304 --> 00:14:03.406
- Left! Left!


00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.476
Left, right, left!


00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:07.577
To the rear! March!


00:14:07.611 --> 00:14:09.514
Left! Left!


00:14:09.547 --> 00:14:11.915
Left, right, left!


00:14:11.948 --> 00:14:14.185
Left! Left!


00:14:14.218 --> 00:14:16.587
Left, right, left!


00:14:16.621 --> 00:14:19.090
Platoon... halt!


00:14:19.123 --> 00:14:22.327
Left... face! At ease.


00:14:22.361 --> 00:14:23.961
Well done, gentlemen.


00:14:23.995 --> 00:14:26.031
You've come a long way.


00:14:26.064 --> 00:14:28.367
You too, Stubby.


00:14:30.502 --> 00:14:34.139
Yep, that's right, it's chow time, boy.


00:14:34.173 --> 00:14:36.608
- Hey, Stubby, look what I got for you.


00:14:36.642 --> 00:14:40.013
- Hey, Biscuit, can we get something other
than dog food?


00:14:40.046 --> 00:14:42.916
- I'm not talking to you, Olsen.
I'm talking to Stubby.


00:14:42.949 --> 00:14:44.484
- How'd you end up here, Schroeder?


00:14:44.517 --> 00:14:46.285
- Yeah, you're a German, ain't you?


00:14:46.318 --> 00:14:49.956
- If a German doesn't join,
he's a slacker.


00:14:49.989 --> 00:14:51.958
- A what?
- A draft dodger.


00:14:51.991 --> 00:14:53.993
- That's why you joined?


00:14:54.027 --> 00:14:57.163
- Do you see how people look
at Germans these days?


00:14:57.197 --> 00:15:00.334
If I didn't join, I couldn't
call myself an American.


00:15:00.367 --> 00:15:04.338
- Hey, you know what, Schroeder?
You're an American in my book.


00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:08.676
- I've watched you men become soldiers.


00:15:08.709 --> 00:15:11.078
It makes me proud.


00:15:11.111 --> 00:15:15.350
You, too, Stubby. I hope you stay on the base
to look after the next group.


00:15:15.383 --> 00:15:19.054
Now, take this weekend to write your letters
and say your farewells.


00:15:19.087 --> 00:15:22.657
And pray that we will all be home safe
and sound very soon.


00:15:22.691 --> 00:15:24.527
We ship out on Monday night.


00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.461
God bless us all,


00:15:26.494 --> 00:15:29.999
and God bless the United States of America.


00:15:30.032 --> 00:15:32.235
Take charge of your men, Sergeant.


00:15:36.406 --> 00:15:38.507
- Dismissed!


00:15:47.117 --> 00:15:51.154
Robert told me how scared he
and the other boys were


00:15:51.188 --> 00:15:55.325
when they finally got word
it was time to go to war.


00:15:57.595 --> 00:16:00.665
I didn't receive Robert's last letter
from camp


00:16:00.698 --> 00:16:02.967
until he had set sail for France.


00:16:03.001 --> 00:16:05.570
He wrote me how about he would miss me,


00:16:05.603 --> 00:16:08.740
but mainly how heartbroken he was


00:16:08.774 --> 00:16:12.110
at having to leave Stubby behind.


00:16:12.143 --> 00:16:14.446
- Not right now, Stubby.


00:16:17.783 --> 00:16:19.752
- I was just so worried,


00:16:19.785 --> 00:16:22.421
and I prayed he'd be home again soon.


00:16:26.259 --> 00:16:28.260
- Stubby, come on.


00:16:28.294 --> 00:16:30.196
This is really hard for me, too.


00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:34.367
You're going to be fine and
I will be back before you know it.


00:16:36.269 --> 00:16:39.039
Look. I got a gift for you.


00:16:58.293 --> 00:17:00.329
Hold it, Stubby, hold it!


00:17:02.196 --> 00:17:05.133
Look at you, handsome boy!
Looking good, Stubby!


00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:10.506
Come now.


00:17:17.213 --> 00:17:20.550
- Like I promised, he'll be here for you
when you get back.


00:17:20.583 --> 00:17:23.454
I'll take good care of him.


00:17:24.320 --> 00:17:27.224
- You hear that, Stubby?
You'll never be hungry.


00:17:57.356 --> 00:17:59.224
Stay, Stubby.


00:17:59.257 --> 00:18:01.494
Don't worry, I'll be back soon.


00:18:01.527 --> 00:18:03.863
Conroy, get in line. Let's go.


00:18:38.332 --> 00:18:40.401
- Stubby! Where you going?!


00:20:05.792 --> 00:20:08.363
- Up, up, up, up, up.


00:20:08.396 --> 00:20:11.933
Bring that up. Hey. Hey,
watch your head over there.


00:20:11.966 --> 00:20:15.002
Hey, hey, hey, hey, hey.


00:22:17.533 --> 00:22:18.901
- Oh!


00:22:18.934 --> 00:22:20.970
Stubby?


00:22:21.003 --> 00:22:24.141
How did you get here? Where did you come from?
How did you get here?


00:22:24.174 --> 00:22:26.108
What are we going to do with you?


00:22:26.142 --> 00:22:28.078
We'll have to hide you.


00:22:28.111 --> 00:22:29.646
Somehow.


00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:31.548
- Oh, brother, it's the mutt.


00:22:31.581 --> 00:22:33.483
How did he get here?


00:22:33.517 --> 00:22:35.853
- I don't know. It's a miracle.


00:22:35.886 --> 00:22:39.089
Stubby, you're just so clever, aren't you?


00:22:40.158 --> 00:22:43.594
- This is a really dumb idea.


00:22:43.628 --> 00:22:45.830
- Shhh, Stubby.


00:22:45.863 --> 00:22:48.766
Shhh. We can't let them hear you.


00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:52.071
- When they find him, they're gonna
throw him overboard.


00:22:57.209 --> 00:22:59.844
- Who in the...?! What...?


00:22:59.877 --> 00:23:01.814
What the...?


00:23:01.847 --> 00:23:04.783
What the devil is going on here?!


00:23:12.091 --> 00:23:14.794
- Ahem-hem. Better salute, sir.


00:23:16.596 --> 00:23:18.832
- Someone's got a lot of explaining to do.


00:23:18.865 --> 00:23:21.102
Who smuggled this dog aboard?


00:23:21.135 --> 00:23:23.470
- He followed me on the train, sir!


00:23:23.503 --> 00:23:24.872
He was quite determined.


00:23:24.905 --> 00:23:27.607
But I swear I did not carry him on board, sir.


00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:31.045
- He sort of became the regiment's
unofficial mascot, sir.


00:23:34.181 --> 00:23:37.486
I didn't carry him on board, either, sir.


00:23:40.755 --> 00:23:43.225
- You're quite resourceful,
aren't you, boy?


00:23:43.258 --> 00:23:45.994
Why, thank you, fella.


00:23:46.027 --> 00:23:47.796
Well, I guess it's better


00:23:47.829 --> 00:23:51.533
to have every man and every dog
we can get in this fight.


00:23:51.566 --> 00:23:55.637
Make sure he receives some... dog tags.
Carry on.


00:23:57.205 --> 00:24:01.610
- Mother! How does he keep
getting away with this?!


00:24:10.187 --> 00:24:13.923
- There ya go, Stubby.
You're a real Yankee now.


00:24:16.927 --> 00:24:21.198
Every day, we scanned the newspapers
for news of the Minnesota.


00:24:21.231 --> 00:24:25.102
They were full of stories about
our boys being lost at sea,


00:24:25.135 --> 00:24:27.572
but nothing about the Yankee Division.


00:24:27.605 --> 00:24:31.543
I had no idea that
Stubby had made it onboard.


00:24:40.819 --> 00:24:43.022
It was two long months
before I got word


00:24:43.055 --> 00:24:46.658
that Robert and Stubby
were safely in France...


00:24:47.159 --> 00:24:49.595
...when I heard they
were headed into action


00:24:49.628 --> 00:24:51.997
at a place called Chemin des Dames,


00:24:52.031 --> 00:24:55.235
where they were going
to join the French lines.


00:25:20.695 --> 00:25:22.296
The Americans.


00:25:35.844 --> 00:25:39.114
- Mother, they look like they've been
through the wringer.


00:25:39.147 --> 00:25:41.684
- That's why we're here, I guess.


00:25:41.718 --> 00:25:43.218
- Shhh!


00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:31.837
Evidently, Stubby wasted no time
in making himself useful.


00:26:31.871 --> 00:26:35.641
He began by clearing the vermin
from the trenches.


00:26:39.779 --> 00:26:42.916
- I'm hungry.


00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:45.418
Somebody tell me there's breakfast.


00:26:59.968 --> 00:27:02.269
- Incoming! Take cover!


00:27:02.737 --> 00:27:05.073
Get down. Get down!


00:27:05.106 --> 00:27:07.042
Hey, get down!
- Oh!


00:27:07.076 --> 00:27:10.678
- Medic!


00:27:10.712 --> 00:27:13.082
Medic!
- Keep down!


00:27:16.485 --> 00:27:18.687
Attention!


00:27:30.499 --> 00:27:32.802
From his first day in action,


00:27:32.836 --> 00:27:34.804
Stubby was on a mission.


00:27:34.838 --> 00:27:36.973
- Medic!!


00:27:37.007 --> 00:27:40.244
- And he dug out many soldiers
who'd been buried.


00:27:42.345 --> 00:27:44.315
Robert was so proud of him.


00:27:47.885 --> 00:27:51.990
- Injured man coming through!
- To your positions! You must hold them!


00:27:52.024 --> 00:27:55.359
Stay on the wall and watch for an attack!


00:27:58.863 --> 00:28:01.033
Come, Sergeant, look.


00:28:04.036 --> 00:28:05.804
- I'll be darned.


00:28:08.408 --> 00:28:11.777
- Okay, but we must not
let down our guard.


00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:16.114
This time, it was only a little welcome
from our Boche friends.


00:28:19.452 --> 00:28:22.255
- Move along, Yanks, move along.


00:28:22.288 --> 00:28:25.192
You cannot save us if you
hang around in here.


00:28:25.225 --> 00:28:27.126
Come on. Next.


00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:29.530
- I don't think they like us.


00:28:29.563 --> 00:28:31.765
I thought "Yankee" was a good word.


00:28:31.799 --> 00:28:34.101
- Excuse me, uh,
something for my dog, please.


00:28:34.134 --> 00:28:36.036
- For your dog?


00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:38.173
Are you crazy, Yankee?


00:28:38.206 --> 00:28:41.810
- He is a soldier.
He's the division mascot.


00:28:41.843 --> 00:28:44.511
Name's Stubby.
You see his dog tags?


00:28:44.545 --> 00:28:47.148
- And now a Boche is telling me


00:28:47.182 --> 00:28:50.385
to give good French food
to an American dog?


00:28:50.418 --> 00:28:53.154
Ah, this war is getting
crazier every day.


00:28:53.188 --> 00:28:55.224
- I am an American.


00:28:55.257 --> 00:28:57.893
- Oh, ja, ja. Of course you are.


00:28:57.927 --> 00:28:59.895
Yeah.


00:28:59.929 --> 00:29:02.565
- He is American! Do you understand?


00:29:02.598 --> 00:29:05.067
- Well, he sounds like a Boche to me.


00:29:05.101 --> 00:29:07.169
- And you sound like a moron, froggy!


00:29:07.203 --> 00:29:09.538
- Quoi?!
- Ow!


00:29:09.572 --> 00:29:12.275
- Enough, you two.


00:29:12.309 --> 00:29:15.011
We are here to fight the Boche,
not each other.


00:29:15.045 --> 00:29:17.047
Allez.


00:29:17.080 --> 00:29:18.915
Un bisou.


00:29:21.618 --> 00:29:24.087
Now, zat's better.


00:29:24.121 --> 00:29:27.091
We are all friend now. Uh?


00:29:27.125 --> 00:29:28.926
- Stubby, shhh.
Calm down. Calm down.


00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:30.360
Come here. Calm down, Stubby.


00:29:30.393 --> 00:29:34.098
- American soldier.
You must be Conroy, no?


00:29:34.132 --> 00:29:36.434
- Yes. I'm-I'm Conroy.


00:29:36.467 --> 00:29:39.905
- I am Gaston Baptiste of the Third Regiment,


00:29:39.938 --> 00:29:42.039
and we are to be working together.


00:29:42.073 --> 00:29:45.611
My Capitaine says, "Look for a man
with a little dog."


00:29:45.644 --> 00:29:47.346
- Hmm. Really?


00:29:47.379 --> 00:29:50.516
- Yes, you and me and your,
uh, little... friend...


00:29:52.484 --> 00:29:55.154
...you will get so close to the Boche,


00:29:55.187 --> 00:29:57.457
you will be able to smell them.


00:30:05.165 --> 00:30:07.968
- Ah!
- Have you ever used one of these?


00:30:08.002 --> 00:30:09.536
Hmm?


00:30:09.569 --> 00:30:12.339
- Um, y-yeah. Yeah, in basic training.


00:30:14.308 --> 00:30:16.977
- It's also perfect for cheese.


00:30:17.011 --> 00:30:19.213
Want to try some?


00:30:20.315 --> 00:30:24.653
I must apologize for this slop
that cook calls food.


00:30:24.686 --> 00:30:29.224
- It's war. I understand
- No, there is no excuse!


00:30:29.257 --> 00:30:33.195
Napoleon say an army marches
on its stomach,


00:30:33.228 --> 00:30:37.233
so they should give ours
with good food.


00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:40.268
Voila.


00:30:40.302 --> 00:30:43.371
- Um... so what exactly
are we going to be doing?


00:30:43.405 --> 00:30:45.508
- Can you ride a horse? No?


00:30:45.541 --> 00:30:48.511
- Yes, yes. I-I mean, I'm...
I might not be the best,


00:30:48.544 --> 00:30:50.513
but you know, I-I'm okay.


00:30:50.546 --> 00:30:53.016
- Good, because our officers have decided


00:30:53.050 --> 00:30:56.185
that we will be spying on the Boche.


00:30:56.219 --> 00:30:59.590
- Hmm?
- You, me and, uh...


00:30:59.623 --> 00:31:02.092
Salty.
- Actually, it's Stubby


00:31:02.125 --> 00:31:05.563
- Okay. But first, let's celebrate.


00:31:07.397 --> 00:31:09.534
- Uh... no.


00:31:09.567 --> 00:31:11.368
- No, mais it's only wine.


00:31:11.401 --> 00:31:13.605
You don't like wine, American soldier?


00:31:13.638 --> 00:31:16.207
- I never drank wine,


00:31:16.241 --> 00:31:18.710
and I'm sure it's against
the rules for us.


00:31:18.743 --> 00:31:20.645
- The rules? What rules?


00:31:20.678 --> 00:31:22.548
This is war.


00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:27.553
Ah. Without wine,


00:31:27.586 --> 00:31:31.056
we French would not have survived
these three years,


00:31:31.090 --> 00:31:32.725
so they give it to us.


00:31:32.758 --> 00:31:35.060
- The French army gives you wine?


00:31:35.094 --> 00:31:37.096
- Bien sûr, mon ami!


00:31:37.129 --> 00:31:41.533
We French go from the milk of our mothers
to the juice of the vine.


00:31:41.567 --> 00:31:43.970
- Hey, you hear that, Stubby?


00:31:44.004 --> 00:31:46.673
They get wine.
- Eh, okay, let's go.


00:31:52.279 --> 00:31:54.749
- Hey, Conroy, Conroy. What's happening?


00:31:54.782 --> 00:31:58.551
- Uh, apparently, I have to follow him


00:31:58.585 --> 00:32:00.254
- Eh. Lucky you.


00:32:00.288 --> 00:32:02.957
- And you two, come with me.


00:32:18.607 --> 00:32:20.542
- This is horrible.


00:32:21.643 --> 00:32:23.479
Everything's destroyed.


00:32:24.647 --> 00:32:26.281
Everything.


00:32:26.315 --> 00:32:28.651
- No, not everything


00:32:32.722 --> 00:32:35.358
- Stubby, down!


00:32:38.561 --> 00:32:40.497
- Come, my friends.


00:32:40.531 --> 00:32:42.198
Those are ours.


00:32:42.231 --> 00:32:44.535
We pay them back for this morning.


00:32:44.568 --> 00:32:46.737
- I thought we were being attacked.


00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:49.206
- You will soon know the difference


00:32:49.239 --> 00:32:51.508
between theirs and ours.


00:33:08.694 --> 00:33:10.229
Et voilà.


00:33:10.261 --> 00:33:13.065
Here it is, your new home.


00:33:16.167 --> 00:33:17.803
Sweet dreams.


00:33:17.837 --> 00:33:20.673
- Get up now, everybody!


00:33:20.706 --> 00:33:22.508
Everybody, get up!


00:33:22.541 --> 00:33:24.744
- Come on, Conroy.


00:33:29.216 --> 00:33:33.353
- Now let me sleep Stubby,
just let me sleep a little more.


00:33:33.387 --> 00:33:35.155
- Woof-woof.


00:33:35.188 --> 00:33:38.192
- Oh. Oh! Ah! Ah. Sorry.


00:33:38.226 --> 00:33:41.161
- Alright, get up now.
- Yeah, I'm up, I'm up.


00:33:41.194 --> 00:33:42.730
I'm up. I'm getting up.


00:33:58.747 --> 00:34:02.117
Uh... have you done this before?


00:34:02.151 --> 00:34:06.489
- Don't worry, it will be fine.
I've done this many times.


00:34:07.556 --> 00:34:09.291
Where did you find him?


00:34:09.324 --> 00:34:12.195
- Oh, uh, he was living rough.


00:34:12.228 --> 00:34:14.229
He joined us when we were training.


00:34:14.263 --> 00:34:16.332
- He's not very beautiful.


00:34:16.366 --> 00:34:18.233
- Oh. Pfft.


00:34:18.267 --> 00:34:22.472
Maybe not, but he's got real character.
- Just like me.


00:34:22.505 --> 00:34:25.375
I'm not the most beautiful,


00:34:25.408 --> 00:34:28.345
but I'm strong and lovable.


00:34:28.379 --> 00:34:31.182
- Uh-huh.


00:34:31.215 --> 00:34:35.453
- This is the place. We get
a good view from here.


00:35:01.981 --> 00:35:03.615
- What do you see?


00:35:10.990 --> 00:35:13.227
- Look.


00:35:13.827 --> 00:35:15.428
It's gas.


00:35:15.461 --> 00:35:18.599
We need to sound the alarm.
Let's go.


00:35:28.308 --> 00:35:30.778
- Halt!
- Run! Run!!


00:35:30.812 --> 00:35:33.515
- Stop!


00:35:33.548 --> 00:35:36.317
I said stop! Halt!


00:35:40.021 --> 00:35:43.691
- The horses! Stay here! Stay!
- Stay, stay! Come on!


00:35:43.725 --> 00:35:45.628
Stubby, go!


00:35:45.661 --> 00:35:48.297
Go tell them, Stubby, go!


00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:22.467
- What? Stubby? Okay! Gas!


00:36:22.501 --> 00:36:24.468
Gas!


00:36:24.502 --> 00:36:25.870
Gas!!!


00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:32.277
- Move it, move it, move it!


00:36:32.311 --> 00:36:34.913
- Let's go, let's go! Get a move on!


00:36:40.385 --> 00:36:41.920
- Oh. Ah.


00:36:48.561 --> 00:36:50.664
- Feuer!!


00:37:10.551 --> 00:37:12.087
- Hey, little dog.


00:37:15.490 --> 00:37:17.392
- Oh. Oh, no.


00:37:17.425 --> 00:37:20.495
- Gas? Gas! Look, gas!


00:37:20.528 --> 00:37:22.797
The dog is trying to warn us!


00:37:22.831 --> 00:37:26.101
Everybody, go inside and
put on your masks!


00:37:30.606 --> 00:37:32.942
- There he is! Stubby!


00:37:45.155 --> 00:37:48.492
Oh, no, we don't have a mask for him!
What are we gonna do?


00:37:48.525 --> 00:37:51.094
Ah, there, there.


00:37:51.128 --> 00:37:52.964
Hold on. Wait.


00:37:52.997 --> 00:37:55.432
- It's okay, Stubby. It's okay.
Good boy.


00:37:55.465 --> 00:37:58.102
You've done well.
- Allez, calme-toi. Calme-toi.


00:38:14.753 --> 00:38:17.523
It's okay. Shhh.


00:38:17.556 --> 00:38:20.392
Stubby, it's gonna be okay. Shhh.


00:39:01.069 --> 00:39:03.505
- Ça va?
- Ça va.


00:39:03.538 --> 00:39:05.006
- Hey!


00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:07.442
Come here. Come here, boy.


00:39:08.243 --> 00:39:10.212
Thank you for my whole village!


00:39:10.245 --> 00:39:11.948
- His name is Stubby.


00:39:11.981 --> 00:39:15.618
- Ah, Stubby. You are our hero, Stubby.


00:39:17.186 --> 00:39:19.222
Robert's letters were full


00:39:19.255 --> 00:39:22.158
of just how beloved Stubby was by everyone.


00:39:24.961 --> 00:39:27.598
- Merci.
- Merci à toi.


00:39:27.631 --> 00:39:33.137
It was as if he lived every day through
the heroics of this little canine wonder.


00:39:33.170 --> 00:39:34.772
- Merci.


00:39:34.805 --> 00:39:37.141
- Yeah, but they were so generous.


00:39:37.174 --> 00:39:39.644
- Yes, yes, but no meat.


00:39:45.884 --> 00:39:48.920
Mm, I think this war has just improved.


00:39:50.588 --> 00:39:53.091
Mm! Like a magician,


00:39:53.124 --> 00:39:56.996
I'm creating magic with a simple rabbit.


00:39:58.763 --> 00:40:02.101
- Allez, Olsen. Now you throw the cochonnet.


00:40:02.134 --> 00:40:04.103
- The what?
- This.


00:40:04.136 --> 00:40:05.938
Not too far, not too close.


00:40:05.971 --> 00:40:08.041
- Bon, bon, bon, bon, bon.
I explain.


00:40:08.074 --> 00:40:11.644
It must land within six to 10 metres


00:40:11.677 --> 00:40:14.114
of the starting circle.


00:40:14.147 --> 00:40:16.983
- Allez, Olsen, now you throw the cochonnet.


00:40:17.017 --> 00:40:19.019
- Okay.
- You can do it, Olsen.


00:40:23.857 --> 00:40:25.993
- Sacre bleu! Rends-nous le cochonnet!


00:40:26.027 --> 00:40:28.996
Give us the cochonnet, cabot!
Sale petit chien!


00:40:29.030 --> 00:40:30.932
- Stubby...


00:40:30.965 --> 00:40:33.634
Drop it, Stubby. Drop it.


00:40:35.069 --> 00:40:36.571
- Ah, merci!


00:40:36.604 --> 00:40:39.307
- No, Stubby, you can't take the cochonnet.


00:40:39.340 --> 00:40:43.744
Pétanque is a very serious business
in this country.


00:40:43.778 --> 00:40:46.081
War has started for less.


00:40:46.114 --> 00:40:48.717
Sorry. He just...
he loves to play ball.


00:40:48.750 --> 00:40:51.019
- Mm! Ta-dah. C'est prêt!


00:40:52.221 --> 00:40:54.290
Now we are ready to eat.


00:40:55.357 --> 00:40:58.761
Ah, excuse me for my bad manners.


00:40:58.795 --> 00:41:01.063
It's your rabbit, too, Stubby.


00:41:04.767 --> 00:41:08.905
- Mm. Whoa! This...
this is really good.


00:41:09.974 --> 00:41:12.041
- Yes, is very tasty, huh?


00:41:12.075 --> 00:41:14.612
Even if I say myself.


00:41:15.412 --> 00:41:19.149
À moi le point!
- Okay.


00:41:19.183 --> 00:41:21.185
Now, if you want to score the point -


00:41:21.218 --> 00:41:24.088
you see that ball there? - hit it hard.


00:41:24.121 --> 00:41:29.059
- You know, we should take a little
after-dinner stroll, while we still can.


00:41:29.093 --> 00:41:30.829
- Yeah, sure.


00:41:30.862 --> 00:41:32.597
C'mon, Stubby.


00:41:42.642 --> 00:41:45.611
- Nice shot.


00:41:53.252 --> 00:41:54.955
- Okay, alright.


00:42:05.332 --> 00:42:08.835
- I have no tobacco, but I carry it
everywhere with me.


00:42:08.869 --> 00:42:11.138
It was a present from my wife.


00:42:12.407 --> 00:42:15.142
I never showed you my family.


00:42:15.777 --> 00:42:17.946
- Wow, three girls.
- Yes.


00:42:17.979 --> 00:42:21.182
Jeanne. She's 11. Manon, eight.


00:42:21.215 --> 00:42:23.284
And Madeleine, six.


00:42:23.318 --> 00:42:25.420
They capture my heart completely.


00:42:25.453 --> 00:42:28.991
They would love to play with you, Stubby.


00:42:29.024 --> 00:42:32.795
They would take you into the forest,
chasing the sanglier.


00:42:32.828 --> 00:42:34.330
- Yeah, sanglier.


00:42:34.363 --> 00:42:36.266
Uh, what... what are they?


00:42:36.299 --> 00:42:39.835
- Wild pigs. You know, with the tusks.


00:42:39.868 --> 00:42:41.805
- Oh, oh. Oh, I get it.


00:42:41.838 --> 00:42:44.239
Your daughters chase wild pigs?


00:42:44.274 --> 00:42:48.043
- Yes, one day, they will make fine hunters.
- Really?


00:42:48.077 --> 00:42:50.146
- Uh, and what about you?


00:42:50.180 --> 00:42:52.248
- Uh...


00:42:52.282 --> 00:42:56.052
My sister Margaret is like a mom to me
and my two younger sisters.


00:42:56.086 --> 00:42:58.723
She cared for our mother before she passed,


00:42:58.756 --> 00:43:01.459
and my dad passed away when I was five.


00:43:01.492 --> 00:43:04.930
I do hope to have a wife and children one day.


00:43:04.963 --> 00:43:08.899
- It is the best. My wife Alice,
she's, uh "how you say?"


00:43:08.933 --> 00:43:13.305
nagging me all the time,
but I wouldn't change her.


00:43:14.139 --> 00:43:18.010
- When did you last see them?
- It is more than two years.


00:43:18.043 --> 00:43:19.778
- Two years?!


00:43:19.811 --> 00:43:23.148
Wow, that's a darned long time.


00:43:23.181 --> 00:43:24.850
Don't you ever get lonely?


00:43:24.883 --> 00:43:27.020
- Yes. Well, sometimes.


00:43:27.053 --> 00:43:33.092
But now you Americans are here,
it will be over soon, you know.


00:43:33.126 --> 00:43:36.195
- Two kings! I win!


00:43:36.229 --> 00:43:40.400
Look, I have two kings! Ha-ha!
I beat you, Yankee.


00:43:40.433 --> 00:43:44.038
- Hold on a minute, hold...
hold on a minute.


00:43:44.071 --> 00:43:47.441
I have a flush, so... I won.


00:43:47.474 --> 00:43:50.344
- Flush? Flush, flush,
flush, flush, flush...


00:43:50.377 --> 00:43:52.346
What is this flush?


00:43:52.379 --> 00:43:54.949
Surely the kings are the kings,
you know, the best!


00:43:57.685 --> 00:44:00.355
- So, uh... before you were a soldier,
what did you do?


00:44:00.388 --> 00:44:04.359
- I'm a chef in my own restaurant.
A good restaurant.


00:44:04.392 --> 00:44:07.196
- Ah! That's why you're such a good cook.


00:44:07.229 --> 00:44:10.365
A real French chef cooking dinner
for us, Stubby.


00:44:10.398 --> 00:44:12.802
What do you think of that, huh?


00:44:12.835 --> 00:44:15.071
- This is how I learn my English.


00:44:15.104 --> 00:44:17.173
And you, what do you do?


00:44:17.206 --> 00:44:20.843
- I work in a builder's hardware store,
uh, selling screws and nails.


00:44:20.876 --> 00:44:23.447
Mm, it's not a great job, but...
it's home.


00:44:23.480 --> 00:44:25.247
I miss it.


00:44:25.281 --> 00:44:28.552
- We French make home wherever we are.


00:44:28.585 --> 00:44:32.122
It's about food, wine and good company.


00:44:33.290 --> 00:44:36.226
You know, on a night like this,


00:44:36.259 --> 00:44:38.895
we can forget about the war.


00:44:46.037 --> 00:44:48.172
We heard the attack and came
as fast as we could!


00:44:48.206 --> 00:44:49.841
- Where do you want us to be, Sergeant?


00:44:49.874 --> 00:44:52.044
- We've beaten them back for now,


00:44:52.077 --> 00:44:55.948
but Clayborn is still somewhere out there.
Gotta bring him in somehow.


00:44:55.981 --> 00:44:58.183
- We got wounded! We got wounded!


00:44:58.217 --> 00:45:00.986
Coming through, guys, coming through!
Mind your backs!


00:45:01.019 --> 00:45:04.423
- Stubby! - Come on, let's go!
- Where you going, Conroy?!


00:45:28.883 --> 00:45:31.452
- Ah, I can't see anything.


00:45:31.485 --> 00:45:34.455
Stubby!!


00:45:41.429 --> 00:45:43.565
- Oh...


00:45:46.501 --> 00:45:48.870
Oh...


00:45:48.904 --> 00:45:50.605
- Get down!


00:45:53.108 --> 00:45:56.044
- Smoke at 7-5-0 yards!


00:46:03.319 --> 00:46:05.655
- Come on, go, go, go, go, go!


00:46:07.490 --> 00:46:10.427
I realized that every day spelled danger...


00:46:10.460 --> 00:46:13.229
there was no letup for our boys.


00:46:18.669 --> 00:46:22.240
- Come on. We're coming through.
- Go, go, go, go, go. You're gonna be okay.


00:46:22.273 --> 00:46:25.475
You're gonna be alright. It's alright.
You're gonna be fine.


00:46:27.312 --> 00:46:28.880
- Thank you, Stubby.


00:46:32.417 --> 00:46:35.553
I got word in April of 1918


00:46:35.587 --> 00:46:40.425
the Yankee Division was moving
to a new battle sector at Seicheprey.


00:46:54.506 --> 00:46:56.543
- Look... look at him!


00:46:56.576 --> 00:46:59.211
Mother! How that mutt survived,
I'll never know.


00:46:59.246 --> 00:47:01.681
I was sure the Boche would get him.


00:47:01.715 --> 00:47:03.483
He is so lucky.


00:47:03.516 --> 00:47:05.085
- He's so brave.


00:47:05.118 --> 00:47:07.955
I wish I had half his courage.


00:47:07.989 --> 00:47:09.456
- Courage? It's not courage.


00:47:09.489 --> 00:47:11.592
It's dumb luck. Believe me.


00:47:14.594 --> 00:47:16.631
- Monsieur?
- Hey, Stubby.


00:47:16.664 --> 00:47:19.333
- Monsieur Conroy, please.


00:47:20.301 --> 00:47:21.736
- Oh. Ah.


00:47:21.769 --> 00:47:23.604
Uh... sorry. Hello, ma'am
- Hello.


00:47:23.638 --> 00:47:27.508
It is for Stubby. We made it
for him to keep him warm


00:47:27.541 --> 00:47:30.746
and to say thank you
for the day of the gas.


00:47:30.779 --> 00:47:34.149
- Ah, gee, thank you. Merci beaucoup.


00:47:39.354 --> 00:47:42.225
Stubby, what do you say to this nice lady?


00:47:42.258 --> 00:47:46.329
No, no, not like that. You know.


00:47:49.465 --> 00:47:52.770
- You are such a clever little dog, Stubby.


00:47:52.803 --> 00:47:54.604
- Oh, I forgot.


00:47:54.637 --> 00:47:58.208
I have something for Stubby
my sergeant made for him.


00:48:00.143 --> 00:48:04.348
Don't be like that, my little friend.
This will save your life.


00:48:08.619 --> 00:48:12.389
- Thank you, Gaston. And
thank you again, ma'am.


00:48:13.425 --> 00:48:16.061
- Goodbye, Stubby and...


00:48:16.094 --> 00:48:17.729
Monsieur Conroy.


00:48:18.730 --> 00:48:23.068
Take good care of yourselves.
- We'll take care of one another.


00:48:23.101 --> 00:48:25.805
- Oui, comme les Trois Mousquetaires
All for one...


00:48:25.838 --> 00:48:27.707
- ...and one for all!


00:48:29.709 --> 00:48:34.180
- Well, I think it's you she likes,
Conroy, no?


00:48:41.220 --> 00:48:44.291
Robert, Stubby and Gaston
spent the next few months


00:48:44.324 --> 00:48:48.128
in the Sibille trench, right in
the path of the German advance


00:48:48.161 --> 00:48:51.064
as they desperately tried
to move on Paris.


00:48:52.499 --> 00:48:56.236
Every soldier, cook and medic
took part in the fight.


00:48:56.870 --> 00:49:00.274
I had no idea that Seicheprey
would be described


00:49:00.308 --> 00:49:03.811
as the single-largest American battle
of World War I.


00:49:03.845 --> 00:49:06.447
And my poor kid brother and his dog


00:49:06.481 --> 00:49:09.317
were smack dab in the middle of it all.


00:49:10.118 --> 00:49:13.554
- A welcome sight you are, Stubby.


00:49:13.588 --> 00:49:15.424
Whoa, whoa, whoa there, fella.


00:49:15.457 --> 00:49:18.127
- I think he remembers you, Colonel, sir.


00:49:18.893 --> 00:49:20.863
- Now, no more reconnaissance
for the moment.


00:49:20.896 --> 00:49:22.664
The Germans are pressing us hard,


00:49:22.698 --> 00:49:25.834
so I need every man reinforcing the line.


00:49:26.702 --> 00:49:30.140
- Yes, sir!
- À vos ordres, mon colonel.


00:49:53.431 --> 00:49:56.435
- What is it? What's going on? What happened?
Calm down. Calm down!


00:50:26.333 --> 00:50:27.900
- Come on, come on, come on.


00:50:31.371 --> 00:50:33.740
- Conroy, watch out!


00:50:35.375 --> 00:50:38.612
Come on, follow me.


00:50:43.685 --> 00:50:46.721
Now let's set a trap for these rats.


00:50:57.733 --> 00:50:59.568
- Stubby!


00:51:13.849 --> 00:51:15.752
- Hande hoch!


00:51:17.554 --> 00:51:19.556
Drop your weapons!


00:51:19.589 --> 00:51:22.760
- Kamerad! Kamerad.


00:51:22.793 --> 00:51:24.628
- No, you are not my kamerad.


00:51:24.661 --> 00:51:27.231
You are my prisoner.
Keep your hands up.


00:51:27.265 --> 00:51:29.466
Good work, my friend.


00:51:29.499 --> 00:51:31.736
I will take them now.


00:51:31.769 --> 00:51:33.271
Go.


00:51:35.873 --> 00:51:39.278
- You're such a brave boy,
you know that?


00:51:39.311 --> 00:51:41.646
- It's not over, boys!
Keep your eyes peeled!


00:51:41.679 --> 00:51:43.415
They'll be back!


00:51:53.358 --> 00:51:54.927
- Ah!!


00:51:54.960 --> 00:51:56.529
- Stubby, no!!


00:51:57.963 --> 00:51:59.699
Stubby!


00:51:59.732 --> 00:52:02.468
- Anybody hurt?! Any wounded?!


00:52:04.837 --> 00:52:07.708
- Stubby! Stubby.


00:52:07.741 --> 00:52:12.446
Stubby! Oh, come on. Stubby?
You'll be okay. Come on.


00:52:12.479 --> 00:52:17.284
Come on, come on, come on. You'll be okay,
you're gonna be okay. Come on.


00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:21.689
Alright. Come on, Stubby!


00:52:21.722 --> 00:52:24.893
Please stay with me. Come on.
Please stay with me.


00:52:27.328 --> 00:52:30.565
- Schroeder, Schroeder, d'you see
what that dumb dog did?


00:52:30.599 --> 00:52:32.299
He saved my life.


00:52:32.334 --> 00:52:34.469
- I need a medic now!
- D'you hear me, Schroeder?


00:52:34.502 --> 00:52:36.472
Wounded man here!


00:52:36.505 --> 00:52:38.406
Quickly, we need help!


00:52:38.440 --> 00:52:41.611
- It's gonna be okay, boy.
I promise. I promise.


00:52:59.529 --> 00:53:01.431
- Hey, Conroy.


00:53:03.634 --> 00:53:05.469
Stubby.


00:53:05.502 --> 00:53:09.941
Stubby, what have they done to you?
Come on.


00:53:11.708 --> 00:53:14.545
- Doc! Doc, Stubby needs help.
- That's a bad wound.


00:53:14.579 --> 00:53:17.414
I don't know if there's much we can do,


00:53:17.447 --> 00:53:19.617
and he's a dog, soldier. I'm sorry.


00:53:19.651 --> 00:53:22.987
- He's my friend, he's one of us!


00:53:23.021 --> 00:53:26.025
He saved lives. Please. Please, Doc.


00:53:26.058 --> 00:53:27.760
Please.


00:53:27.793 --> 00:53:31.964
- I'm sure the doctor will do his best.
Right, Doc?


00:53:31.997 --> 00:53:35.567
- Okay, okay. Just lay him
on the ambulance.


00:53:40.539 --> 00:53:43.043
- You come back soon, little dog.


00:53:43.076 --> 00:53:45.477
We need our third musketeer.


00:53:46.211 --> 00:53:50.383
- Guys, make way! Move!
Alright, hurry, hurry!


00:53:50.417 --> 00:53:52.719
- Schroeder?


00:53:52.752 --> 00:53:55.389
- Let's keep moving, keep moving.


00:53:55.423 --> 00:53:57.424
Alright, get that, yes.


00:54:05.032 --> 00:54:07.703
- Don't worry, Conroy. He's strong.


00:54:16.445 --> 00:54:18.714
- Great day to...


00:54:19.781 --> 00:54:22.417
...be an American, right, Stubby?


00:54:26.621 --> 00:54:30.193
After Schroeder and Stubby were wounded,


00:54:30.226 --> 00:54:33.596
the fighting around
Seicheprey became ferocious.


00:54:33.629 --> 00:54:35.933
And the rains came down.


00:54:35.966 --> 00:54:39.669
It rained day after day after day,


00:54:39.702 --> 00:54:43.439
turning the whole place
into a quagmire.


00:54:46.410 --> 00:54:50.147
I can only begin to imagine
how Robert missed Stubby.


00:54:50.881 --> 00:54:54.752
He must've felt totally lost
without the little fella.


00:54:58.989 --> 00:55:02.694
- Come on, Conroy. I know it's hard,


00:55:02.727 --> 00:55:04.863
but you must stay strong.


00:55:04.896 --> 00:55:06.898
- German attack! Let's go!


00:55:07.799 --> 00:55:10.101
The Yankee Division was driven out


00:55:10.135 --> 00:55:12.972
of the Sibille trench and
the town of Seicheprey.


00:55:13.005 --> 00:55:16.074
As the Germans desperately
tried to reach Paris,


00:55:16.107 --> 00:55:20.546
all that stood between them
and their prize were our boys.


00:55:20.579 --> 00:55:24.116
It was a real worrying time
for all of us back home.


00:55:25.118 --> 00:55:27.020
- They mean business.


00:55:27.820 --> 00:55:30.157
We need to stop them.


00:55:30.190 --> 00:55:32.691
No, not now, Musketeer.


00:55:32.725 --> 00:55:35.462
Tomorrow. First, we need to rest.


00:55:36.930 --> 00:55:40.567
I miss him too, but
I know the Three Musketeers


00:55:40.600 --> 00:55:42.703
will be together again very soon.


00:55:42.737 --> 00:55:44.671
I feel it.


00:55:44.704 --> 00:55:46.741
- You think?


00:55:46.774 --> 00:55:50.645
- Yes. And the good thing is,


00:55:50.678 --> 00:55:55.516
we are out of those trenches,
my feet are dry at last.


00:55:55.550 --> 00:55:57.785
All that mud and those rats,


00:55:57.819 --> 00:55:59.521
Argh. Dégueulasse.


00:55:59.555 --> 00:56:01.789
- Baptiste?


00:56:01.823 --> 00:56:05.260
You've been ordered to return to your regiment camp.
Grab your gear and move out.


00:56:08.897 --> 00:56:11.601
- Stubby, Schroeder, and now you?


00:56:11.634 --> 00:56:13.936
So much for your predictions.


00:56:13.969 --> 00:56:15.872
- You'll be fine.


00:56:15.905 --> 00:56:18.842
But, before I leave, just take my advice:


00:56:18.875 --> 00:56:21.845
To stay alive, you need to feel fear.


00:56:21.878 --> 00:56:23.813
- Fear?


00:56:23.847 --> 00:56:26.549
But you show no fear. You laugh!


00:56:26.583 --> 00:56:28.819
- You mean this?


00:56:28.853 --> 00:56:32.089
It only protects my fragile heart.


00:56:35.960 --> 00:56:37.928
Farewell, my good friend.


00:56:38.929 --> 00:56:40.764
Until we meet again.


00:56:42.199 --> 00:56:43.869
- Adieu.


00:56:43.902 --> 00:56:47.639
♪ La Madelon vient nous servir à boire ♪


00:56:47.672 --> 00:56:51.577
♪ Sous la tonnelle elle frôle son jupon ♪


00:56:51.611 --> 00:56:55.748
♪ Et chacun lui raconte une histoire ♪


00:56:55.781 --> 00:56:58.817
♪ Une histoire à sa façon ♪


00:56:58.850 --> 00:57:00.686
Adieu, mon ami.


00:57:02.121 --> 00:57:03.923
After much hard fighting,


00:57:03.956 --> 00:57:07.760
our boys retook Seicheprey
and drove the Germans back.


00:57:07.793 --> 00:57:09.762
It was not until much later


00:57:09.795 --> 00:57:13.000
that I learned just how fierce
the battle had been.


00:57:13.600 --> 00:57:16.069
My dear sister Margaret,


00:57:16.103 --> 00:57:19.206
I hope you and the girls are keeping well.


00:57:19.239 --> 00:57:21.675
I'm doing just fine.


00:57:28.650 --> 00:57:31.119
Even the chow is okay.


00:57:31.152 --> 00:57:33.021
Well, not like your cooking,


00:57:33.054 --> 00:57:35.290
but pretty much most of it I can eat.


00:57:37.059 --> 00:57:40.128
It's almost four weeks since Stubby got hit


00:57:40.161 --> 00:57:42.965
and there's still no news of him.


00:57:42.997 --> 00:57:45.768
I'm sure he's being taken real good care of.


00:57:45.801 --> 00:57:48.137
Gaston, Stubby and me,


00:57:48.170 --> 00:57:50.639
we called ourselves The Three Musketeers.


00:57:50.673 --> 00:57:53.310
You know, like in the book Mom read to us


00:57:53.343 --> 00:57:55.110
when we were kids?


00:57:55.144 --> 00:57:58.148
Well, the other day...


00:57:58.181 --> 00:58:01.116
Gaston was sent back to his regiment.


00:58:01.150 --> 00:58:04.120
It's all going to be over soon,


00:58:04.154 --> 00:58:06.656
and then we'll be coming home.


00:58:06.690 --> 00:58:08.992
I send my love to you all.


00:58:09.025 --> 00:58:12.829
Your loving brother, Robert.


00:58:12.862 --> 00:58:15.967
Robert was always good at putting
a brave face on,


00:58:16.000 --> 00:58:18.869
and I knew that was what he was doing.


00:58:19.670 --> 00:58:22.140
His two best friends gone.


00:58:22.174 --> 00:58:24.675
My heart went out to him.


00:59:32.181 --> 00:59:35.384
- Conroy, wake up. It's guard time.
We'll miss chow.


00:59:36.986 --> 00:59:38.421
Conroy?


00:59:39.922 --> 00:59:42.091
Oh, no.


00:59:42.124 --> 00:59:45.228
Quick, we need a doctor!
My buddy is real sick!


00:59:58.876 --> 01:00:01.379
- Was?
- Ein hund!


01:00:10.488 --> 01:00:12.457
- He's the fifth soldier this week.


01:00:12.490 --> 01:00:14.293
I don't know what it is.


01:00:14.326 --> 01:00:17.830
Just gotta keep an eye on him.
He's too sick to move.


01:00:17.863 --> 01:00:20.132
- But he is gonna make it, right, Doc?


01:00:20.165 --> 01:00:23.502
- I don't know. Maybe... maybe not.


01:00:24.303 --> 01:00:29.174
In 1918, the whole world was seized
by a deadly flu epidemic,


01:00:29.208 --> 01:00:31.478
and Robert caught it too.


01:00:31.511 --> 01:00:34.447
It spread through our boys
like a poison cloud.


01:00:43.991 --> 01:00:47.228
- Ah! I gotta get out there and fight.
I need to get up.


01:00:47.261 --> 01:00:49.130
- We're good, Conroy.
We're good, we're good.


01:00:55.003 --> 01:00:57.505
Everything's good.
The Boche are real quiet.


01:00:57.538 --> 01:00:59.341
Just get better.


01:00:59.374 --> 01:01:01.909
- Mm... Stubby.


01:01:01.942 --> 01:01:04.346
Where's... Stubby?


01:01:04.379 --> 01:01:07.482
- Just let him rest. That's
the only thing he can do now.


01:01:28.071 --> 01:01:29.939
- I... I need to fight.


01:03:01.771 --> 01:03:06.075
I was so happy to hear that
Robert was on the mend,


01:03:06.109 --> 01:03:08.311
and that he and Stubby were reunited.


01:03:10.614 --> 01:03:13.616
And good news! After six months
of hard fighting,


01:03:13.650 --> 01:03:16.620
he and Stubby went on a furlough to Paris.


01:03:16.654 --> 01:03:21.125
- Never imagined my first trip
to Paris would be in a cattle wagon.


01:03:21.158 --> 01:03:22.761
So glamorous!
- I remember thinking:


01:03:22.794 --> 01:03:26.398
Maybe this dreadful war was finally
coming to an end.


01:03:26.431 --> 01:03:29.600
- You're gonna have to lay down
at some stage.


01:03:33.705 --> 01:03:35.473
- Room for one more?


01:03:35.507 --> 01:03:37.776
- Hey, buddy, you made it!
- Schroeder!


01:03:37.809 --> 01:03:40.378
Stubby, look, he's back!


01:03:40.412 --> 01:03:43.783
- Stubby, you're here!
What happened to you?


01:03:43.816 --> 01:03:47.320
- Uh, hello? Why is it always
about Stubby?


01:03:47.353 --> 01:03:49.322
I found him. "Thank you, Olsen,


01:03:49.355 --> 01:03:52.091
for finding Stubby. Oh, that was great!"


01:03:53.359 --> 01:03:55.595
- Sorry, Olsen. Come here!


01:04:13.748 --> 01:04:15.783
- This looks nice.


01:04:22.190 --> 01:04:24.259
What do you think, Stubby?


01:04:25.626 --> 01:04:28.831
- You coming, Schroeder?
- Yep, I'm with you!


01:05:01.499 --> 01:05:05.736
In the beginning, the French
were very suspicious of Americans,


01:05:05.770 --> 01:05:08.806
but by the fall of 1918, they loved us.


01:05:08.839 --> 01:05:10.608
Bravo!


01:05:16.614 --> 01:05:20.252
- Bravo aux Américains.
- Thank you. Thank you very much.


01:05:20.285 --> 01:05:22.354
- Bravo aux Américains!


01:05:23.288 --> 01:05:25.558
- Merci. Thanks.


01:05:29.227 --> 01:05:33.131
Come on, let's go see Paris!
What are we waiting for?


01:06:08.670 --> 01:06:10.940
By September of 1918,


01:06:10.973 --> 01:06:15.277
we were told that the war
was coming to an end,


01:06:15.311 --> 01:06:18.314
and that the German army
was in retreat.


01:06:21.684 --> 01:06:24.787
- What is it, Stubby?


01:06:24.820 --> 01:06:28.391
Stubby. Stubby, come back.


01:06:36.433 --> 01:06:38.535
- Ah!


01:06:38.568 --> 01:06:39.937
Ah!!


01:06:43.507 --> 01:06:45.743
No!


01:06:46.611 --> 01:06:49.847
- Don't even think about it.


01:06:54.585 --> 01:06:56.288
This is yours now, Stubby.


01:06:56.321 --> 01:06:58.389
You earned it.


01:06:58.422 --> 01:07:00.926
- Well, look what Stubby sniffed out.


01:07:01.994 --> 01:07:04.296
- Kamerad!


01:07:04.329 --> 01:07:05.964
- Move it!
- No! Ah!


01:07:10.035 --> 01:07:13.673
- Come on.


01:07:14.374 --> 01:07:17.944
- We are here today to mark the courage


01:07:17.977 --> 01:07:19.979
of a very brave soldier


01:07:20.013 --> 01:07:22.682
who went above and beyond
the call of duty.


01:07:22.715 --> 01:07:26.253
Private First Class Conroy
and Stubby, post!


01:07:28.956 --> 01:07:32.559
- For his capture of a German spy
in Marcheville,


01:07:32.592 --> 01:07:36.030
I confer the honorary rank
of Sergeant on Stubby,


01:07:36.063 --> 01:07:37.865
the regimental mascot.


01:07:37.898 --> 01:07:39.633
From this day forward,


01:07:39.667 --> 01:07:42.504
Sergeant Stubby will wear these stripes.


01:07:42.537 --> 01:07:47.275
And for you, Private Conroy,
today you are Corporal Conroy.


01:07:51.479 --> 01:07:54.283
- Hey, Stubby, you outrank me now.


01:07:59.455 --> 01:08:00.922
Gaston!


01:08:04.994 --> 01:08:08.898
- For the newspapers back home!
Say cheese!


01:08:10.934 --> 01:08:13.069
The newspaper headlines


01:08:13.102 --> 01:08:16.039
all talked about a final assault
on the Hindenburg line.


01:08:19.810 --> 01:08:21.845
After the return of his friends,


01:08:21.878 --> 01:08:24.982
Robert's letters were cheery
and full of good humour.


01:08:25.015 --> 01:08:29.920
He even told me a funny story
about how Stubby came face-to-face


01:08:29.953 --> 01:08:31.889
with a strange new beast of war.


01:08:37.962 --> 01:08:39.732
- Okay, Sergeant,


01:08:39.765 --> 01:08:44.069
I'm Captain George S. Patton
of the United States Tank Corps.


01:08:46.404 --> 01:08:48.407
Let's go.


01:08:48.440 --> 01:08:50.342
We've got a war to win.


01:09:00.687 --> 01:09:02.489
- That's my boy.


01:09:35.023 --> 01:09:37.459
I can't believe it.


01:09:37.492 --> 01:09:40.930
The Germans built this like
they were going to stay forever.


01:09:40.963 --> 01:09:43.065
- The Boche build palaces


01:09:43.098 --> 01:09:46.502
and we had to live in those puddles?


01:09:46.536 --> 01:09:48.905
- It's over, it's over! Did you hear?


01:09:48.938 --> 01:09:50.506
The war is over!


01:09:50.540 --> 01:09:52.175
The Germans are signing an armistice.


01:09:52.208 --> 01:09:55.611
Says who?


01:09:55.645 --> 01:09:58.615
- It's true! The war is to end tomorrow!


01:09:58.649 --> 01:10:00.616
- Hey, did you hear that, Stubby?


01:10:09.861 --> 01:10:11.895
- Hold on, hold on there, soldiers!


01:10:11.929 --> 01:10:15.533
Cease-fire doesn't start till 11 a.m.!


01:10:15.567 --> 01:10:19.437
- Germany surrendered!
- When is it over? - We have a war to fight!


01:10:24.876 --> 01:10:28.546
- You know, guys, when I get home,
I'm gonna find myself a wife.


01:10:28.580 --> 01:10:31.150
- I thought you were going to wait
until you found a job.


01:10:31.183 --> 01:10:35.454
- Nah, after all we've been through,
I'm ready for some home life.


01:10:35.488 --> 01:10:38.925
- Me, too. Can't wait
to introduce Stubby to my sisters.


01:10:38.959 --> 01:10:40.960
- I've been away so long,


01:10:40.993 --> 01:10:45.498
I worry my girls will not recognize me.


01:10:45.531 --> 01:10:47.868
- Why can't we just leave now?


01:10:47.901 --> 01:10:51.805
- Just think, one last push and
then we're all going home.


01:10:53.273 --> 01:10:56.610
- Well, little buddy,
we're nearly there.


01:10:56.643 --> 01:11:00.648
Just a few more hours and...
it'll all be over.


01:11:10.826 --> 01:11:15.197
Robert and Stubby spent their
last night of the Great War


01:11:15.230 --> 01:11:17.633
huddled together under an oak tree.


01:11:17.666 --> 01:11:19.268
A man and his dog,


01:11:19.301 --> 01:11:21.637
unselfish friends,


01:11:21.670 --> 01:11:24.040
comrades in arms.


01:12:54.303 --> 01:12:57.705
- Drop your weapons!


01:13:37.448 --> 01:13:39.718
Thank you, my friend.


01:13:43.355 --> 01:13:46.290
I never did understand why our boys


01:13:46.324 --> 01:13:50.963
were made to attack until
the very last minute of the war.


01:13:50.996 --> 01:13:54.333
So many young men were lost
on that morning,


01:13:54.366 --> 01:13:58.036
but they did their duty...
to the very end.


01:13:58.070 --> 01:13:59.739
- We made it.


01:14:28.102 --> 01:14:30.071
- Olsen!


01:14:30.105 --> 01:14:32.272
Olsen, where are you?!


01:14:32.306 --> 01:14:34.075
Where are you?


01:15:34.306 --> 01:15:38.010
- Well, my friends,
we have seen much together,


01:15:38.043 --> 01:15:41.780
and now the Three Musketeers
must say goodbye.


01:15:46.318 --> 01:15:48.288
Stubby!


01:15:48.321 --> 01:15:50.824
Yes, good boy.


01:15:50.857 --> 01:15:53.059
- My friend Olsen gave these to me.


01:15:53.093 --> 01:15:56.296
I will always think of him
when I play poker.


01:15:56.329 --> 01:15:58.064
Take this and think of us.


01:15:58.665 --> 01:16:01.335
Goodbye, Yankees, and thank you all.


01:16:05.506 --> 01:16:07.942
- All for one!
- And one for all!


01:16:10.344 --> 01:16:12.314
- Vive la France!


01:16:12.347 --> 01:16:14.348
- Vive les États-Unis!


01:16:22.357 --> 01:16:25.827
- We're so proud of you!
- Welcome!


01:16:37.206 --> 01:16:39.877
- Welcome home, boys.
- Welcome back!


01:16:39.910 --> 01:16:41.911
- Great job.


01:16:42.645 --> 01:16:45.916
- It's so nice to have you back!


01:16:46.617 --> 01:16:48.552
- Whoo!
- Robert!


01:17:13.546 --> 01:17:16.281
- A hero! You're a hero, Stubby!


01:17:32.498 --> 01:17:35.268
- Stubby, look into the camera!


01:17:35.302 --> 01:17:37.638
And... hold it right there.

